WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.200
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4402.

2
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.200
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.960
<v LENORIA AMBROSE>Wonderful.

4
00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:01.680
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Yes.

5
00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.907
<v AMBROSE>I remember just before . . . Well I guess I’d better not say that either.

6
00:00:03.907 --> 00:00:05.120
I’d better hold and wait [laughs].

7
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.260
<v CHARLOTTE WILLCOX>We can start.

8
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:06.460
Go ahead.

9
00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:06.900
<v ARSENEAULT>We’re good?

10
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.100
Okay.

11
00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:11.460
Today is January twenty-third, 2015.

12
00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:17.600
And I’m Chelsea Arseneault representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

13
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:23.740
We’re working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum and Bill Sherman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

14
00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:30.880
And to kick that off, we are holding a Mossville oral history day here at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

15
00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.520
I’m here today with Ms. Lenoria Ambrose.

16
00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:36.080
What a beautiful name.

17
00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.820
Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed.

18
00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:40.060
This is going to be a short interview, about fifteen minutes,

19
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:45.760
and we will likely be in touch with you later to set up a much longer interview if you’re interested in continuing on.

20
00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:46.620
<v AMBROSE>I am.

21
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:47.520
<v ARSENEAULT>Great!

22
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.200
Just for the record could you state your full name?

23
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:54.100
<v AMBROSE>My name is Lenoria Marie Braxton Ambrose.

24
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:54.347
<v ARSENEAULT>Great.

25
00:00:54.347 --> 00:00:58.120
And when and where were you born?

26
00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:01.120
<v AMBROSE>I was born in Lake Charles, Louisiana at the Saint Patrick’s Hospital,

27
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:09.980
but I was immediately brought to Mossville where I remained until I married at the age of eighteen and moved to Lake Charles.

28
00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:13.000
But my roots are here in Mossville.

29
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.680
<v ARSENEAULT>Great.

30
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:22.220
What are your parents’ names?

31
00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:22.820
<v AMBROSE>My mother’s name is Gloria Jean Towner Harris.

32
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:27.080
My father’s name is Gradis Braxton.

33
00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.787
<v ARSENEAULT>Could you spell Gradis?

34
00:01:29.787 --> 00:01:34.280
<v AMBROSE>G-R-A-D-I-S.

35
00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.160
<v ARSENEAULT>And your grandparents’ names?

36
00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:51.420
<v AMBROSE>My mother’s father’s name is William Arthur Towner, and her mother’s name is [Birdie?] Mae Perkins Towner.

37
00:01:51.420 --> 00:02:02.060
My father’s father’s name is Lee Braxton, and his mother’s name was [Oretha?] Rigmaiden Braxton.

38
00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:07.600
<v ARSENEAULT>Thank you so much for that.

39
00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.700
<v AMBROSE>I can go back further if you need it [laughs].

40
00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:12.476
<v ARSENEAULT>I’ve noticed there’s a lot of Rigmaidens and Braxtons.

41
00:02:12.476 --> 00:02:13.900
That name is . . .

42
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:18.040
<v AMBROSE>But the Perkins and the Mosses the original originals.

43
00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:18.240
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

44
00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:25.000
<v AMBROSE>And I don’t know you guys know the story of Mossville where it was called Shoat's Prairie because of all the pigs,

45
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.660
the hogs that they had.

46
00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:37.280
And then . . . and I always told them whenever I would talk about Mossville, I would tell them that I was from Mossville proper.

47
00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:40.500
The railroad track . . . There used to be a sign that said “Mossville.”

48
00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:47.100
Anything from that railroad track right there all the way back down to . . . past the LaTours' was considered Mossville.

49
00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:53.160
They built the school outside of the Mossville area because that was the largest piece of land that they had.

50
00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:58.260
And of course then they built the Bel Air and Lincoln Heights subdivisions around it,

51
00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.620
but that was the . . . This is the original Mossville.

52
00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:07.100
Then when you went to Evergreen Road going north, which was called Saprack.

53
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.380
And the Braxtons, the LaDouxs, the Rigmaidens,

54
00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:10.920
<c.p>they were all up there.

55
00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:16.120
And down on my end was the Mosses . . . well on my . . . on the Perkins end was the Mosses and the Perkins.

56
00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.680
<v ARSENEAULT>Got you.

57
00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:19.960
<v AMBROSE>And it was called Perkinstown.

58
00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:29.610
So before it was Mossville, it was Perkinstown, which I’m from that end, and Saprack, which was the north . . . north end.

59
00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:33.710
<v ARSENEAULT>So do you know why your family came to Mossville?

60
00:03:33.710 --> 00:03:38.050
<v AMBROSE>From the history that we have for the Perkins and the Mosses, they were already here.

61
00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:42.330
They were in the . . . something they called Little Woods or Big Woods.

62
00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:54.470
Which was . . . As close as I can come to the actual location was in the Edgerly area, Vinton over Sulphur, in that area.

63
00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:55.730
They were on a farm there.

64
00:03:55.730 --> 00:04:05.450
And then they had the Homestead Act of 1862, and they came . . . Why they chose this area?

65
00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:17.260
I have no history of that, but I do know that my great great grandmother, Minerva Moss Perkins, homesteaded a hundred and sixty-two acres.

66
00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:22.740
That’s how the Perkins came here, and the Mosses did the same thing.

67
00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:26.820
And the . . . She was a Moss, and married a Perkins.

68
00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:29.780
So both of those families were connected.

69
00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:40.000
And then Griffin Braxton, he homesteaded a hundred and sixty-two acres up on Evergreen Road, so.

70
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:40.860
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s a lot of land.

71
00:04:40.860 --> 00:04:49.930
What is your earliest memory of Mossville?

72
00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:51.550
<v AMBROSE>School.

73
00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:53.410
Going to the old school.

74
00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:56.270
And I’m hoping to find a picture of it.

75
00:04:56.270 --> 00:05:03.205
It was just this big humongous one story schoolroom with a high, high ceiling.

76
00:05:03.205 --> 00:05:05.710
I guess as tall as these ceilings.

77
00:05:05.710 --> 00:05:08.870
And we had first, second, and third grades in it.

78
00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:13.110
And then there was another building off to the side that was the cafeteria,

79
00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:22.190
and on the other side . . . It was on this piece of property, in fact, was the junior high.

80
00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:28.450
And during the time that I was coming to school here, if you wanted high school you had to be sent to Lake Charles,

81
00:05:28.450 --> 00:05:32.430
to one of the schools in Lake Charles, because that was the nearest high school that we had.

82
00:05:32.430 --> 00:05:34.910
<v ARSENEAULT>So the original school was on this . . .?

83
00:05:34.910 --> 00:05:35.710
<v AMBROSE>On this site.

84
00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:37.650
<v ARSENEAULT>The Rigmaiden Recreational site?

85
00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:38.110
<v AMBROSE>[Agrees].

86
00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:40.050
<v ARSENEAULT>So . . . And it was just first, second, and third?

87
00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:44.370
<v AMBROSE>It was first, second, third in one building, and then they had like junior high . . .

88
00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:44.570
<v ARSENEAULT>Got you.

89
00:05:44.370 --> 00:05:49.390
<v AMBROSE>Over in the other building.

90
00:05:49.390 --> 00:05:51.710
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you live growing up?

91
00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:53.670
<v AMBROSE>In the Mossville area.

92
00:05:53.670 --> 00:05:59.090
I lived down on the west end of Mossville in the Perkinstown area.

93
00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:01.730
<v ARSENEAULT>Could you describe the house and the property?

94
00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:05.590
<v AMBROSE>It’s funny because just recently I was looking in a magazine,

95
00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:12.010
and this magazine had . . . It was called a dog run, or a foxtrot house, are you familiar with that?

96
00:06:12.010 --> 00:06:14.410
Where you have the porch with the breezeway in between?

97
00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:21.610
Well when my grandfather bought property he . . . It was on this side of the highway,

98
00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:28.270
the Old Spanish Trail, I guess about a mile down east . . . west of here.

99
00:06:28.270 --> 00:06:34.930
Our house had a living room and a bedroom, was the first house.

100
00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:36.910
Then there was a breezeway between.

101
00:06:36.910 --> 00:06:43.830
And then there was a kitchen, which was a big kitchen, big enough to put a couch in, and another bedroom.

102
00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:49.510
And Daddy always . . . And then at the back part there was a two bedroom that was detached.

103
00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:53.510
And we always asking, “Well Daddy . . .” My grandfather said, “Why didn’t you put it together?”

104
00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:57.350
He said, “Well,” he said, “When I bought the . . . Bought them,”

105
00:06:57.350 --> 00:07:01.630
he said, “I wanted to have a breezeway where we could sit like in the evening.”

106
00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:08.690
And he said, “I didn’t want the kitchen to be attached to the other part of the house because it would be hot.”

107
00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:10.310
And then he said,

108
00:07:10.310 --> 00:07:15.930
“Well I had these boys and I didn’t want them when they grew up to have to wake everybody up when they . . . Their

109
00:07:15.930 --> 00:07:19.510
<c.p>comings and goings,” so he said, “I put them out in the back.”

110
00:07:19.510 --> 00:07:23.110
So we had our own foxtrot or dog run house and didn’t know it.

111
00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:23.310
Didn’t know that there was a name for it.

112
00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:27.250
<v ARSENEAULT>So there was a logical reason probably why this . . .

113
00:07:27.250 --> 00:07:27.790
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

114
00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:28.870
And he never put it together.

115
00:07:28.870 --> 00:07:32.290
He kept them separate and that was his reasoning for doing that.

116
00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:35.370
So I said well that’s . . . I never even thought about that.

117
00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:42.710
And then when I was reading about this style of house because I . . . Before they started buying out this area I wanted to move back here for the weekend.

118
00:07:42.710 --> 00:07:46.110
I belong to church here, at Mount Zion Baptist Church.

119
00:07:46.110 --> 00:07:50.190
And I wanted to leave my house on the weekends and come over here, stay all weekend,

120
00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:56.790
and then go back home for the rest of the week because I hate coming back and forth across the bridge to church.

121
00:07:56.790 --> 00:08:02.950
And I would come over in the morning at nine o’clock and I would stay until . . . If BTU was at six,

122
00:08:02.950 --> 00:08:06.230
I would stay with my uncle who lived down the street until six o’clock.

123
00:08:06.230 --> 00:08:08.710
And then I would go to church, and then I would go home.

124
00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:10.030
I’d say, well we have this property here.

125
00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:16.750
I could just build a house, a small house, and just stay here on the weekends and then go home on Monday.

126
00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:21.410
And then when my family comes here from California, they can stay over here the whole time, so.

127
00:08:21.410 --> 00:08:22.570
That was my intentions.

128
00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:25.010
And now I’m kind of up in the air.

129
00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:30.550
I want to see how many people are going to be left in Mossville before I make the final arrangements of what I’m going to do.

130
00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:32.190
But I was going to build a house like that.

131
00:08:32.190 --> 00:08:34.710
A small house like the old house that we had.

132
00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:35.630
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y’all have a garden?

133
00:08:35.630 --> 00:08:40.630
<v AMBROSE>Always had a garden, always had animals, always had chickens.

134
00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:44.050
And my uncle . . . I had an uncle, my mother’s brother,

135
00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:51.150
who always had . . . We had a cow, he had pigs, and we always had horses.

136
00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:56.010
In fact, when my uncles went to California, they took their horses with them.

137
00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:58.990
Yes, we always had animals.

138
00:08:58.990 --> 00:08:59.850
Always.

139
00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:04.870
Dogs, cats . . . I never did like dogs, and never did like cats, but we had them.

140
00:09:04.870 --> 00:09:11.290
And when my grandmother lived we had chickens because she always liked to cook with the eggs, so.

141
00:09:11.290 --> 00:09:15.000
Yes.

142
00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:15.200
<v ARSENEAULT>Great.

143
00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:16.890
So I was going to ask where you went to church, but . . .

144
00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:17.570
<v AMBROSE>Mount Zion.

145
00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:18.670
Mount Zion Baptist Church.

146
00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:19.410
Yes.

147
00:09:19.410 --> 00:09:22.430
<v ARSENEAULT>And who were some of the leaders in the church that you . . .?

148
00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:29.070
<v AMBROSE>Well our church is going to be a hundred and forty nine years old this year.

149
00:09:29.070 --> 00:09:32.250
This is our hundred and forty ninth anniversary.

150
00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:36.850
Eighteen seventy-six, I think that makes it a hundred and forty nine years.

151
00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:44.910
Griffin Braxton, who was my great great great grandfather was one of the first preachers there.

152
00:09:44.910 --> 00:09:52.810
And my great great grandfather, Isom Perkins was one of the first deacons.

153
00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:58.950
And Isom Perkins was married to Minerva Moss, one of the first Mosses that was here.

154
00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:08.580
And of course all of my uncles on the Perkins side belonged to Mount Zion,

155
00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:19.030
and they all served as secretaries, or deacons, or some form of office in the church.

156
00:10:19.030 --> 00:10:20.610
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did you go to school?

157
00:10:20.610 --> 00:10:22.490
<v AMBROSE>At Mossville High School.

158
00:10:22.490 --> 00:10:24.310
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any teachers?

159
00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:24.650
<v AMBROSE>Oh, gosh.

160
00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:26.050
Yes.

161
00:10:26.050 --> 00:10:30.910
When they built Mossville [School] in 1956, I was in fourth grade.

162
00:10:30.910 --> 00:10:36.910
And my fourth grade teacher was Mrs. Armstrong.

163
00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:38.070
Ethel Armstrong.

164
00:10:38.070 --> 00:10:45.810
And my fifth grade teacher was Ethel Mitchell.

165
00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:53.730
And then of course in the sixth grade we changed classes, so I had a bunch of teachers in the sixth grade.

166
00:10:53.730 --> 00:10:58.470
But some of the teachers that I remember really really well didn’t actually teach me,

167
00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:05.170
but were teachers in the elementary school were Zella Wiley, or [Battel?] at the time.

168
00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:09.510
We have a . . . She was Ms. Kilgore, but she married a Rogers.

169
00:11:09.510 --> 00:11:12.310
Became Mrs. Gloria Rogers.

170
00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:15.170
High school English.

171
00:11:15.170 --> 00:11:22.470
And we had . . . Dolores Spikes was my high school chemistry teacher.

172
00:11:22.470 --> 00:11:45.250
My history teacher was Mr. Obie Pete, my high school science teacher was Wilson [Arfay?], and my home economics teacher was Ava Prater.

173
00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:46.850
<v ARSENEAULT>What year did you graduate?

174
00:11:46.850 --> 00:11:49.570
<v AMBROSE>1964.

175
00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:50.947
And I was Miss Mossville.

176
00:11:50.947 --> 00:11:52.580
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh!

177
00:11:52.580 --> 00:11:56.980
I would love to see that picture.

178
00:11:56.980 --> 00:12:04.820
<v AMBROSE>My cousin . . . I had a cousin who lived in Long Beach [California].

179
00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:06.980
When he . . . He was born in California.

180
00:12:06.980 --> 00:12:09.380
Lived in the San Francisco area, but then he moved down to

181
00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:16.760
<c.p>Long Beach and he opened a restaurant there and he named the restaurant 70663, which was the zip code.

182
00:12:16.760 --> 00:12:17.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

183
00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:17.200
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

184
00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:22.320
And the . . . It was based on a lot of Mossville memorabilia.

185
00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:26.540
And I . . . My sister said, “Well we’re going to go to Mac’s restaurant while you’re here,”

186
00:12:26.540 --> 00:12:28.740
she said, “Because I want you to see it before you leave.”

187
00:12:28.740 --> 00:12:30.980
And she said his wife does the music there.

188
00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:36.440
They had jazz every night there and it was just a really quaint little restaurant.

189
00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:42.300
And when I walked in there she didn’t tell me how . . . it was . . . I knew the name of it and that it was named after Mossville,

190
00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:47.560
but when I walked in there, there was all kind of Mossville memorabilia that they had.

191
00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:48.360
They had the jackets.

192
00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:53.640
Somebody sent him a Mossville jacket that the guys wore.

193
00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:59.460
There was pirates . . . it was . . . It had kind of a pirate-y theme because we were the pirates.

194
00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:02.620
And then I look on the wall at all these pictures, and there my picture is.

195
00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:03.380
I said, “Oh my God.

196
00:13:03.380 --> 00:13:03.980
I want my picture.

197
00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:05.760
I want to take it home.

198
00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:08.980
I don’t want anybody to see it!” [Laughing].

199
00:13:08.980 --> 00:13:09.792
<v ARSENEAULT>Is it still up there?

200
00:13:09.792 --> 00:13:10.740
Or does he still have it?

201
00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:16.480
<v AMBROSE>I think the restaurant is . . . he passed and I think his wife shut the restaurant down after he passed.

202
00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:17.280
Yeah.

203
00:13:17.280 --> 00:13:19.180
<v ARSENEAULT>I wonder where that . . . All that memorabilia went.

204
00:13:19.180 --> 00:13:19.900
<v AMBROSE>I don’t know.

205
00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:20.100
<v ARSENEAULT>Hmm.

206
00:13:19.900 --> 00:13:21.540
<v AMBROSE>I don’t know.

207
00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:31.200
I have to ask my sister if she knows anything about what happened because there was a lot of stuff that people had sent him and had given him.

208
00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:39.700
And a thing I want to say too, my grandmother, my great great grandmother Minerva Moss Perkins,

209
00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:59.680
she homesteaded the hundred and sixty-two acres where the acreage across . . . Slightly across Highway 90 up to the Old Spanish Trail and all the way down to the end of where Mossville is up to . . . Just before you get to Coach Williams Road.

210
00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:02.720
That was . . . Her hundred and sixty-two acres were in there.

211
00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:08.400
And she was the first in the community to have glass windows in her house,

212
00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:15.440
and she had shutters on the front of it I guess in case of the storms and what have you.

213
00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:18.460
And her father gave her the windows.

214
00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:23.960
She was half Indian and half white and her dad gave her the windows for her house.

215
00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:30.960
So we were always pleased when we’d go walking through the woods and passed her house we could see the . . . Because it was there for many many years of my growing up.

216
00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:33.980
Going from highway . . . From Old Spanish Trail to Highway 90.

217
00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:38.260
I don’t know if you’re familiar with Highway 90 where you come in . . . Did you guys come in at Prater Road?

218
00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:38.960
<v WILLCOX>Yes.

219
00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:41.620
<v AMBROSE>That’s Highway 90.

220
00:14:41.620 --> 00:14:47.200
That’s Highway 90 and we would just walk through the woods from here to Highway 90.

221
00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:49.280
<v ARSENEAULT>Which places were the most important in Mossville?

222
00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:51.820
The buildings, and parks, and . . .

223
00:14:51.820 --> 00:14:52.580
<v AMBROSE>Our church.

224
00:14:52.580 --> 00:14:53.420
All the churches.

225
00:14:53.420 --> 00:15:04.100
We had Mount Zion Baptist Church, we had a Methodist church, we had a Sanctified church, or Apostolic church.

226
00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:09.900
And then we had other churches that came in later like the . . . In fact,

227
00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:18.680
just down the street . . . I’m sure some of my family have probably given you that history already, of the Mossville Tabernacle of Faith.

228
00:15:18.680 --> 00:15:22.340
That’s on my great grandfather’s property, on the Towner’s.

229
00:15:22.340 --> 00:15:24.680
And I haven’t told you anything about the Towners yet,

230
00:15:24.680 --> 00:15:28.000
but I have a whole book over there that I put together for our . . . the Towner history.

231
00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.520
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, wonderful.

232
00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:31.960
That will definitely be something for a follow up interview.

233
00:15:31.960 --> 00:15:32.400
<v AMBROSE>Yeah.

234
00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:33.160
<v ARSENEAULT>For sure.

235
00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:33.360
That’s fantastic.

236
00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:40.620
<v AMBROSE>And they came here from . . . in about 1906 from Kipling, Louisiana.

237
00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:44.832
And my grandfather always . . . and my . . . the Lemelles, have you met any of the Lemelles?

238
00:15:44.832 --> 00:15:46.480
<v ARSENEAULT>I don’t know.

239
00:15:46.480 --> 00:15:46.760
<v AMBROSE>That’s my . . .

240
00:15:46.760 --> 00:15:49.460
<v WILLCOX>[. . .?] mentioned.

241
00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:50.060
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

242
00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:50.440
Well they . . .

243
00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:50.840
<v ARSENEAULT>[. . .?] mentioned.

244
00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:54.380
<v AMBROSE>That’s one . . . She married into the Lemelle family, one of my grandfather’s sisters.

245
00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:58.260
And they always liked to tell you when we’d say Sugartown, it wasn’t Sugartown.

246
00:15:58.260 --> 00:15:59.380
It was Kipling.

247
00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:01.180
And I’ve looked and I can’t find Kipling.

248
00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:07.960
I don’t know if it’s no longer Kipling or maybe I’m not spelling it right, but they were from Kipling, Louisiana.

249
00:16:07.960 --> 00:16:14.140
And I don’t know why . . . And probably for work reasons they left Sugartown.

250
00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:25.980
My grandfather was four years old and his oldest sister . . . His older sister was two years old.

251
00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:26.920
And he . . . My grandfather,

252
00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:33.820
whenever we’d go to DeRidder [Louisiana] to see his family he would mark every place on the side of the road where they stopped and camped.

253
00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:34.887
I said, “Daddy how can you remember that?

254
00:16:34.887 --> 00:16:36.000
You were only four years old.”

255
00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:38.780
He said, “I remember.

256
00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:39.580
I remember.”

257
00:16:39.580 --> 00:16:41.660
And he would tell us every spot.

258
00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:44.980
It took them days to come here because they were in a wagon.

259
00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:47.380
And he likes to tell . . . He liked to tell us that story.

260
00:16:47.380 --> 00:16:50.420
And when they first came here they moved up on Evergreen Road.

261
00:16:50.420 --> 00:16:58.440
They bought . . . My great grandfather bought property up there before he moved down here, but they lived up there for many years, so.

262
00:16:58.440 --> 00:16:59.380
<v ARSENEAULT>So much history.

263
00:16:59.380 --> 00:17:00.660
<v AMBROSE>Yes.

264
00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:01.480
A lot of history.

265
00:17:01.480 --> 00:17:04.540
That’s why I didn’t want them to sell . . . to tear Mossville up.

266
00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:07.060
Because it’s . . . even though I don’t live here anymore,

267
00:17:07.060 --> 00:17:11.680
I came over here for . . . We use this community center for a lot of our family affairs,

268
00:17:11.680 --> 00:17:14.140
and then I had a lot of family still left here.

269
00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:15.400
And it was just . . . I don’t know.

270
00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:19.080
It just broke my heart to see them disrupted.

271
00:17:19.080 --> 00:17:24.180
But we still have our church, so I’m hoping we won’t sell our church.

272
00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:28.460
<v ARSENEAULT>So kind of going off that, what does home mean to you?

273
00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:34.040
<v AMBROSE>It’s my life, it’s my soul, it’s . . . Everything that I know.

274
00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:38.700
I mean I’ve been married, well if my husband had lived we would have been married fifty years next year.

275
00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:42.980
I lived that time in Lake Charles, but still I’m right across the street.

276
00:17:42.980 --> 00:17:46.280
And I worked at the plants for forty years.

277
00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:47.880
And it’s just home.

278
00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:49.940
It’s our lifeblood.

279
00:17:49.940 --> 00:17:50.820
It’s our memories.

280
00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:54.240
It’s everything.

281
00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:56.180
And I just . . . I don’t know.

282
00:17:56.180 --> 00:18:01.540
I guess everybody isn’t connected like we are because we know that we were the first ones here.

283
00:18:01.540 --> 00:18:03.040
My families were the first ones here.

284
00:18:03.040 --> 00:18:10.780
And they made Mossville what it is and they instilled in us a pride of home.

285
00:18:10.780 --> 00:18:22.340
That was one thing they always . . . I can remember from a little bitty kid when they would be telling us things is to always be . . . Your word is your bond always.

286
00:18:22.340 --> 00:18:26.920
Anytime anybody want to make a deal with you, do it with a handshake and keep your promise.

287
00:18:26.920 --> 00:18:28.480
Get an education and learn.

288
00:18:28.480 --> 00:18:37.760
My grandfather had a third grade education and I had a third grade primer and in that . . . And I used to wonder I said,

289
00:18:37.760 --> 00:18:44.040
“Well Daddy only had a third grade education, how could he do math so fast and measure things?”

290
00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:47.080
My grandfather and his brothers were all master carpenters.

291
00:18:47.080 --> 00:18:53.980
And they could just read . . . and my grandfather, if he put something on the wall, it was straight straight straight.

292
00:18:53.980 --> 00:18:55.680
He put a ruler on it.

293
00:18:55.680 --> 00:18:57.680
And I said, “Daddy how do you

294
00:18:57.680 --> 00:18:58.840
<c.p>know all that fractions?”

295
00:18:58.840 --> 00:19:02.920
And when we were learning our time tables and all those kinds of things,

296
00:19:02.920 --> 00:19:08.420
and then I looked at that primer and in third grade they had fractions.

297
00:19:08.420 --> 00:19:10.400
And he learned . . . They learned their time tables.

298
00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:12.520
They learned things they don’t even teach kids nowadays.

299
00:19:12.520 --> 00:19:17.220
I’ve seen so many kids . . . We had a restaurant in Lake Charles and they didn’t even know their time tables.

300
00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:18.460
High schoolers.

301
00:19:18.460 --> 00:19:19.580
They don’t teach it to them.

302
00:19:19.580 --> 00:19:25.260
They think that they’re going to get it just through doing.

303
00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:28.900
But we got it through memorization.

304
00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:31.580
So I said well they had a third grade education,

305
00:19:31.580 --> 00:19:38.720
but probably in today’s times it probably was equal to a sixth grade education . . . seventh grade education.

306
00:19:38.720 --> 00:19:46.060
Because math was one of the . . . they didn’t know a lot about . . . and then they diagram sentences.

307
00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:49.280
Something I had in high school, but kids don’t do that anymore either I don’t think.

308
00:19:49.280 --> 00:19:54.960
So the formal education they had, it might have been only second, or third, or fourth grade educations,

309
00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:59.520
but it was so much in that that they had, which I thought was just priceless.

310
00:19:59.520 --> 00:20:03.040
Priceless.

311
00:20:03.040 --> 00:20:05.100
So Mossville is my home.

312
00:20:05.100 --> 00:20:09.060
And wherever I go, it’ll always be home.

313
00:20:09.060 --> 00:20:10.680
Whether we’re here or not.

314
00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:13.300
<v ARSENEAULT>So what does community mean to you?

315
00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:14.420
<v AMBROSE>Oh, God.

316
00:20:14.420 --> 00:20:21.920
Well when I was growing up, that’s been a long time ago, you know how they say it takes a village to raise?

317
00:20:21.920 --> 00:20:23.960
This was a village.

318
00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:25.560
Everybody knew everybody.

319
00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:27.060
Everybody was related to everybody.

320
00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:33.180
If it wasn’t through blood it was through marriage, or just through a bond of friendship.

321
00:20:33.180 --> 00:20:35.760
And everybody looked out for everybody’s kids.

322
00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:41.440
We always rode the school bus to school, but when there were days that we decided we wanted to walk,

323
00:20:41.440 --> 00:20:46.980
and we would conveniently miss the bus, well we would stop and pick blackberries along the road.

324
00:20:46.980 --> 00:20:47.240
And

325
00:20:47.240 --> 00:20:52.260
<c.p>before we get home my mom knew where we had been, how long it had taken us,

326
00:20:52.260 --> 00:20:57.840
because somebody was watching all the way down the road until we got home.

327
00:20:57.840 --> 00:21:05.020
So it’s a . . . It was a wonderful place to grow up.

328
00:21:05.020 --> 00:21:06.740
A wonderful place to grow up.

329
00:21:06.740 --> 00:21:08.020
We had stores here.

330
00:21:08.020 --> 00:21:10.640
We had black owned stores.

331
00:21:10.640 --> 00:21:14.240
We had a canteen which was . . . Two canteens.

332
00:21:14.240 --> 00:21:20.860
The Lyon’s . . . Lula Lyons had our first canteen which was right across the street.

333
00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:24.620
Then we had . . . The Freelow’s had one down by the school.

334
00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:30.560
So we had . . . There were a lot of businesses that we had in Mossville.

335
00:21:30.560 --> 00:21:32.300
People had grocery stores.

336
00:21:32.300 --> 00:21:36.880
Right across the track on the right hand side there was a grocery store.

337
00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:38.640
Ira Garrett owned that grocery store.

338
00:21:38.640 --> 00:21:46.000
And then right down the street the Rigmaiden . . . Josh Rigmaiden, who donated this land, he had a little grocery store.

339
00:21:46.000 --> 00:21:49.000
So it was always things to do.

340
00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:51.920
And then our churches were . . . We were active in our churches.

341
00:21:51.920 --> 00:21:53.400
There was a lot of activity going on in church.

342
00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:54.060
There was always something.

343
00:21:54.060 --> 00:22:02.380
If there wasn’t eating Sunday dinners on the ground, there were penny marches, and pew rallies, it was just always something to do.

344
00:22:02.380 --> 00:22:05.620
It was a busy little community.

345
00:22:05.620 --> 00:22:07.280
<v ARSENEAULT>Well our time’s almost up.

346
00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:13.760
On behalf of the center and the museum we appreciate your contribution today.

347
00:22:13.760 --> 00:22:16.740
Did you want to say anything else in closing?

348
00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:19.040
<v AMBROSE>No. I’m just glad that they’re doing this.

349
00:22:19.040 --> 00:22:23.340
I just wish they had done it before they started the selling process.

350
00:22:23.340 --> 00:22:26.440
Because it was important, and Mossville is important.

351
00:22:26.440 --> 00:22:28.120
It’s important to a whole lot of people.

352
00:22:28.120 --> 00:22:42.040
And it would have been so much better had everybody been here and we could have . . . We would have had time to prepare a little bit better and get a little bit more information so that they could have had a thorough history because there’s so many important things.

353
00:22:42.040 --> 00:22:45.640
And I don’t know if you guys have been told about the [Rockamore?] Center or

354
00:22:45.640 --> 00:22:50.440
<c.p>the orphan home they had here back in the . . . I don’t know what years it was,

355
00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:55.060
but there was just . . . we . . . it was just a bustling community.

356
00:22:55.060 --> 00:22:57.700
And a lot of people worked here.

357
00:22:57.700 --> 00:22:58.860
If they weren’t farmers,

358
00:22:58.860 --> 00:23:06.680
they were . . . They worked in the sawmills and they worked at the industry once the . . . in 1936 Olin was the first plant here.

359
00:23:06.680 --> 00:23:09.100
And a lot of people worked in industry.

360
00:23:09.100 --> 00:23:15.140
So we’ve just had . . . I just can’t really say anything negative about it.

361
00:23:15.140 --> 00:23:19.000
It’s home.

362
00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:21.210
<v ARSENEAULT>Well thank you so much for sharing that with us.

363
00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:22.250
<v AMBROSE>I’m glad I could share.

364
00:23:22.250 --> 00:23:23.115
I’m glad I could share.

365
00:23:23.115 --> 00:23:25.850
<v ARSENEAULT>We will . . . This concludes the interview.

366
00:23:25.850 --> 00:23:31.170
We’ll be in touch with the possibility of setting up a longer second interview to follow up.

367
00:23:31.170 --> 00:23:32.290
<v AMBROSE>Okay.

368
00:23:32.290 --> 00:23:35.130
<v ARSENEAULT>This is a great place to start definitely.

369
00:23:35.130 --> 00:23:38.000
<v AMBROSE>Well I hope I gave you guys a . . .

370
00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:38.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4402.

371
00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:38.200
End Session I.]
